; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Format strings.
;
; ("fmt" arg ...) takes the FIRST element as the format and everything after
; it as an argument -- adjacent string literals are NOT concatenated the way
; they are in C.  A format spelled as several literals therefore formats the
; second literal's address through the first one's %s and throws the rest
; away.  So any format that will not fit on one line is assembled with cat
; and used through a variable, which the language allows in exactly the same
; position.
; --------------------------------------------------------------------------

(set F_KEEPOLD
  (cat "This machine already has network configuration, including an "
       "interface called \"%s\".\n\n"
       "Keep the existing configuration, or configure the network again?"))

